1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,849
Okay.

2
00:00:01,149 --> 00:00:02,521
I know how this looks.

3
00:00:02,821 --> 00:00:08,457
I'm running through Paris, in nineteen eleven, with the most famous painting in the world under my arm.

4
00:00:08,757 --> 00:00:13,617
Behind me: three policemen, one very excited dog, and a whistle that will not stop.

5
00:00:13,917 --> 00:00:15,215
And in front of me...

6
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:16,992
a cart full of baguettes.

7
00:00:17,192 --> 00:00:17,762
Crunch!

8
00:00:18,062 --> 00:00:22,123
So how did a regular guy in a red cap end up stealing the Mona Lisa?

9
00:00:22,423 --> 00:00:23,494
Funny story.

10
00:00:23,794 --> 00:00:25,007
I didn't steal it.

11
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:26,821
I was trying to save it.

12
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:28,261
Let me back up.

13
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:34,425
It all started in my bedroom.

14
00:00:34,725 --> 00:00:38,785
I was looking for my phone charger in a box of my grandpa's old stuff.

15
00:00:39,085 --> 00:00:41,415
And at the very bottom, I found a ticket.

16
00:00:41,715 --> 00:00:43,614
An old, yellow, paper ticket.

17
00:00:43,914 --> 00:00:45,241
Musée du Louvre.

18
00:00:45,541 --> 00:00:46,286
Paris.

19
00:00:46,586 --> 00:00:48,706
August twenty first, nineteen eleven.

20
00:00:49,006 --> 00:00:49,733
Weird.

21
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:53,216
Then my thumb touched the date, and the ticket started to glow.

22
00:00:53,516 --> 00:00:55,304
The light swallowed my whole room.

23
00:00:55,604 --> 00:01:01,583
My lamp went one way, my socks went the other, and I went spinning through a tunnel of gold and blue light.

24
00:01:01,883 --> 00:01:02,795
Backwards.

25
00:01:03,095 --> 00:01:05,103
More than a hundred years backwards.

26
00:01:05,603 --> 00:01:08,857
I landed on a cobblestone street with a very loud bonk.

27
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:10,081
Horses.

28
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:11,307
Carriages.

29
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:13,212
Gentlemen in top hats.

30
00:01:13,512 --> 00:01:15,911
Ladies with hats the size of umbrellas.

31
00:01:16,211 --> 00:01:18,273
And in the distance, the Eiffel Tower.

32
00:01:18,573 --> 00:01:22,352
Only twenty two years old, and already the most famous thing in Paris.

33
00:01:22,752 --> 00:01:25,998
A newsboy was shouting the headlines, so I grabbed a paper.

34
00:01:26,298 --> 00:01:28,967
Monday, August twenty first, nineteen eleven.

35
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:30,668
My brain did the math.

36
00:01:30,968 --> 00:01:35,348
That was the day somebody walked into the Louvre, and walked out with the Mona Lisa.

37
00:01:35,648 --> 00:01:38,094
And it would take two whole years to get her back.

38
00:01:38,594 --> 00:01:40,270
Normal people would panic.

39
00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:41,791
I panicked too.

40
00:01:42,091 --> 00:01:43,458
Then I made a plan.

41
00:01:43,758 --> 00:01:44,877
Stop the thief.

42
00:01:45,177 --> 00:01:46,404
Save the painting.

43
00:01:46,704 --> 00:01:48,408
Be home in time for dinner.

44
00:01:48,708 --> 00:01:49,456
Easy.

45
00:01:49,856 --> 00:01:50,933
One problem.

46
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,769
On Mondays, the Louvre was closed to visitors.

47
00:01:54,069 --> 00:01:55,123
Staff only.

48
00:01:55,423 --> 00:02:01,719
So I borrowed a white cleaner's coat from a laundry line, grabbed a broom, and tried to look extremely professional.

49
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:03,011
I whistled.

50
00:02:03,311 --> 00:02:04,142
I swept.

51
00:02:04,442 --> 00:02:05,914
I nodded at the guard.

52
00:02:06,214 --> 00:02:07,392
He nodded back.

53
00:02:07,692 --> 00:02:08,591
I was in!

54
00:02:08,991 --> 00:02:09,813
And wow.

55
00:02:10,113 --> 00:02:11,458
The Louvre is huge.

56
00:02:11,758 --> 00:02:15,785
Long golden galleries, painted ceilings, marble statues everywhere.

57
00:02:16,085 --> 00:02:19,374
Fun fact: before it was a museum, it was a royal palace.

58
00:02:19,774 --> 00:02:24,635
I swept my way to a famous room called the Salon Carré, where the Mona Lisa hung.

59
00:02:24,935 --> 00:02:26,049
And I froze.

60
00:02:26,349 --> 00:02:30,040
On the wall, between the other paintings, there were just four iron hooks.

61
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:31,732
And an empty space.

62
00:02:32,032 --> 00:02:33,169
I was too late!

63
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:36,130
Then, at the far end of the gallery, I saw him.

64
00:02:36,430 --> 00:02:42,906
A man in a white workman's smock, just like mine, walking very fast, and holding something flat, wrapped in a cloth.

65
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:43,847
Hmm.

66
00:02:44,147 --> 00:02:45,166
Suspicious.

67
00:02:45,566 --> 00:02:46,684
I followed him.

68
00:02:46,984 --> 00:02:48,182
Very sneakily.

69
00:02:48,482 --> 00:02:50,474
I hid behind a Roman statue.

70
00:02:50,774 --> 00:02:52,501
Then behind a Greek statue.

71
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:55,217
Then behind a guard, who was not a statue.

72
00:02:55,517 --> 00:02:56,874
The man turned around.

73
00:02:57,174 --> 00:02:59,340
I froze, and pretended to be art.

74
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:00,466
It worked!

75
00:03:00,766 --> 00:03:02,187
Then he started running.

76
00:03:02,487 --> 00:03:03,549
So did I.

77
00:03:03,849 --> 00:03:08,563
And that's when I discovered that freshly waxed museum floors are extremely slippery.

78
00:03:08,863 --> 00:03:15,968
I slid across the gallery like a hockey puck, bounced off a suit of armor, and tumbled down an entire marble staircase.

79
00:03:16,268 --> 00:03:16,905
Bump.

80
00:03:17,205 --> 00:03:17,841
Bump.

81
00:03:18,141 --> 00:03:18,778
Bump.

82
00:03:19,078 --> 00:03:19,715
Bump.

83
00:03:20,015 --> 00:03:24,096
Right past the Winged Victory of Samothrace, who did not help at all.

84
00:03:24,396 --> 00:03:26,640
And I landed right in front of the thief.

85
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:28,444
We crashed into each other.

86
00:03:28,744 --> 00:03:30,488
The painting flew into the air.

87
00:03:30,788 --> 00:03:32,035
Time slowed down.

88
00:03:32,335 --> 00:03:33,163
I dove.

89
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:34,323
I stretched.

90
00:03:34,623 --> 00:03:35,723
And I caught it!

91
00:03:36,023 --> 00:03:37,472
I peeked under the cloth.

92
00:03:37,772 --> 00:03:38,959
A wooden panel.

93
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:40,626
A mysterious smile.

94
00:03:40,926 --> 00:03:42,105
It was really her!

95
00:03:42,405 --> 00:03:46,370
Fun fact: the Mona Lisa is painted on poplar wood, not canvas.

96
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:47,866
And she's tiny.

97
00:03:48,166 --> 00:03:50,167
About the size of a school poster.

98
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:54,585
I looked up to thank the universe, and the universe sent me a museum guard.

99
00:03:54,885 --> 00:03:56,081
He looked at me.

100
00:03:56,381 --> 00:03:57,762
He looked at the painting.

101
00:03:58,062 --> 00:03:59,637
He looked at the empty wall.

102
00:03:59,937 --> 00:04:01,556
The real thief had vanished.

103
00:04:01,856 --> 00:04:03,492
And the guard blew his whistle.

104
00:04:03,792 --> 00:04:04,419
Thief!

105
00:04:04,719 --> 00:04:05,722
Stop, thief!

106
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:06,987
So I ran.

107
00:04:07,287 --> 00:04:11,441
Out of the Louvre, across the courtyard, and into the busy streets of Paris.

108
00:04:11,741 --> 00:04:13,418
Policemen blowing whistles.

109
00:04:13,718 --> 00:04:15,833
A carriage driver yelling in French.

110
00:04:16,132 --> 00:04:19,786
And a little dog who decided this was the best day of his life.

111
00:04:20,086 --> 00:04:24,640
I jumped over a flower stand, ducked under a horse, and dodged a man on a bicycle.

112
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:27,208
Then I turned the corner, and there it was.

113
00:04:27,508 --> 00:04:28,657
The baguette cart.

114
00:04:28,957 --> 00:04:29,682
Yes.

115
00:04:29,982 --> 00:04:31,162
That baguette cart.

116
00:04:31,462 --> 00:04:32,033
Crunch!

117
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:33,437
Bread everywhere.

118
00:04:33,737 --> 00:04:35,109
The dog was thrilled.

119
00:04:35,409 --> 00:04:36,791
The baker was not.

120
00:04:37,191 --> 00:04:40,493
I hid under a pile of bread until the whistles faded away.

121
00:04:40,793 --> 00:04:42,244
My heart was pounding.

122
00:04:42,544 --> 00:04:49,273
I was holding the most famous painting in the world, and every cop in Paris was looking for a short guy in a red cap.

123
00:04:49,573 --> 00:04:51,095
I needed a new plan.

124
00:04:51,595 --> 00:04:53,824
So I found a pencil, and wrote a note.

125
00:04:54,124 --> 00:04:55,121
Dear police.

126
00:04:55,421 --> 00:04:57,073
Here is the Mona Lisa.

127
00:04:57,373 --> 00:04:58,669
I did not steal it.

128
00:04:58,969 --> 00:04:59,943
I found it.

129
00:05:00,243 --> 00:05:01,971
Please give it back to the Louvre.

130
00:05:02,271 --> 00:05:03,713
Sincerely, a friend.

131
00:05:04,013 --> 00:05:04,776
P.S.

132
00:05:05,076 --> 00:05:06,522
Sorry about the baguettes.

133
00:05:06,922 --> 00:05:12,625
That night, I tiptoed to the police station, placed the wrapped painting on the steps, rang the bell, and ran.

134
00:05:12,925 --> 00:05:20,301
From behind a lamppost, I watched a sleepy officer open the door, look down, and scream so loudly that the whole street woke up.

135
00:05:20,801 --> 00:05:22,204
And that's when it hit me.

136
00:05:22,504 --> 00:05:26,359
In real history, the Mona Lisa disappeared for more than two years.

137
00:05:26,659 --> 00:05:35,855
The thief, a man named Vincenzo Peruggia, hid her in a trunk in his tiny Paris apartment, until he was caught in Florence in December, nineteen thirteen.

138
00:05:36,155 --> 00:05:40,055
Meanwhile, crowds lined up at the Louvre just to stare at the empty wall.

139
00:05:40,355 --> 00:05:43,178
That's how she became the most famous painting on Earth.

140
00:05:43,578 --> 00:05:46,337
And I had just brought her back on the very first night.

141
00:05:46,637 --> 00:05:47,788
Two years early.

142
00:05:48,088 --> 00:05:50,201
Then my ticket started glowing again.

143
00:05:50,701 --> 00:05:51,409
Whoosh.

144
00:05:51,709 --> 00:05:52,990
Back in my bedroom.

145
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:54,623
Same messy floor.

146
00:05:54,923 --> 00:05:56,320
Still no charger.

147
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:59,794
I grabbed my laptop and searched: Mona Lisa theft.

148
00:06:00,094 --> 00:06:01,491
And my jaw dropped.

149
00:06:01,791 --> 00:06:04,414
Stolen August twenty first, nineteen eleven.

150
00:06:04,714 --> 00:06:12,491
Returned the very same night, by an unknown hero, described by witnesses as short, very fast, and wearing a strange red cap.

151
00:06:12,991 --> 00:06:14,060
I was famous!

152
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:15,113
Kind of.

153
00:06:15,413 --> 00:06:18,578
Then the ticket glowed one more time, and new words appeared.

154
00:06:18,878 --> 00:06:20,822
Next destination: Titanic.

155
00:06:21,123 --> 00:06:22,324
Nineteen twelve.

156
00:06:22,624 --> 00:06:23,389
Uh oh.

157
00:06:23,689 --> 00:06:24,343
No.

158
00:06:24,643 --> 00:06:26,022
No, no, no, no.

159
00:06:26,322 --> 00:06:27,532
Not the Titanic!

160
00:06:27,832 --> 00:06:29,608
Can I get a refund on this thing?

161
00:06:30,207 --> 00:06:33,444
If you enjoyed my disaster, subscribe to History World!

162
00:06:33,744 --> 00:06:35,706
And maybe pack me a life jacket.
